<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yeon Gaesomun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 665</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yeon Gaesomun was a Goguryeo general and statesman who seized power in a coup in 642, killing King Yeongnyu and placing King Bojang on the throne while ruling as military dictator. He resisted Tang invasions under Emperor Taizong, including the siege of Ansi fortress in 645. After his death, quarrels among his sons helped the Silla and Tang alliance conquer Goguryeo in 668.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yeon-gaesomun.jpg" alt="Yeon Gaesomun" width="200" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yon_Kaesomun.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yeon Gaesomun was the first son of Yŏn T'aejo, who held the office of Mangniji, comparable to prime minister, under King Pyeongwon and King Yeongyang; his grandfather Yŏn Chayu had held it too. Tang records call him Ch'ŏn, because his family name was also the given name of the Tang founder, Emperor Gaozu, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> calls him Iri Kasumi. Little is known of his early life before he became Western Governor, when he oversaw the building of the Cheolli Jangseong, a chain of garrisons defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> against the Tang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In the winter of 642, learning that King Yeongnyu and his officials were plotting to kill him, he invited about a hundred opponents to a banquet, killed them all and then murdered the king in his palace. He placed the king's nephew Bojang on the throne, took the title Tae Mangniji and ruled Goguryeo until his death. Traditional historians saw in the coup a thirst for power; many modern Korean historians see an attempt to take a harder line against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> than the king had.</p><p>After a brief conciliatory approach to the Tang, including a request in 643 for Taoist teachers, relations broke down when Goguryeo attacked Silla. In 645 Emperor Taizong took several border fortresses but was held for months at Ansi, while Yeon Gaesomun defeated the marine force sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>. Taizong retreated in winter with heavy losses and was defeated again in 647 and 648. Under Emperor Gaozong, Yeon Gaesomun's forces destroyed the army of the Tang general Pang Xiaotai at the Sasu River in 662, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/su-dingfang">Su Dingfang</a> was forced back from Pyongyang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yeon Gaesomun died of natural causes, most likely in 665, the year recorded on the tomb stele of his eldest son Yŏn Namsaeng; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a> gives 666 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> 664. His sons and younger brother then fought over the succession, Namsaeng defecting to Tang, and Goguryeo fell to the Tang and Silla in 668. Tang and Silla sources portrayed him as a brutal and arrogant dictator, and Joseon historians blamed him for Goguryeo's fall, while the nationalist historian Shin Chae-ho placed him above every other hero of Korean history. In Chinese opera and legend he appears as the adversary of the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xue-rengui">Xue Rengui</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yŏn Kaesomun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 642 the general <b>Yeon Gaesomun</b> killed King Yeongnyu and more than a hundred officials, placed Bojang on the throne and ruled as military dictator with the title dae mangniji.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In China warlords such as Dong Zhuo and Cao Cao seized the central government at the end of the Han dynasty, and in Korea the general <b>Yeon Gaesomun</b> took control of Goguryeo in 642 after having its king killed.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
